#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# boolean_counting_function

#% text
# `boolean_counting_function(k, n)` is the Boolean function of `n` arguments that is true when at most `k` of them are true.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `boolean_counting_function([kmin, kmax], n)` (two numbers) is true when between `kmin` and `kmax` of the arguments are true; `boolean_counting_function([k], n)` when exactly `k`, and `boolean_counting_function([[k1, k2, ...]], n)` when exactly `k1`, `k2`, ... of them are.

#% item
# A call with all arguments `true` or `false` gives the value; with some of them it is the function of the others.

#% item
# `boolean_convert` turns a call with symbols into a sum of products or another form.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
boolean_counting_function([2, 3], 4)(true, true, false, true)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
boolean_convert(boolean_counting_function([2, 3], 4)(a, b, c, d))
#% output
#= a and b and not c or a and not b and c or a and not b and d or not a and c and d or b and c and not d or b and not c and d

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# As an expression in variables:

#% cell
boolean_counting_function([2, 3], [a, b, c])
#% output
#= a and b or a and c or b and c

#% text
# Exactly one or three:

#% cell
boolean_table(boolean_counting_function([[1, 3]], 3)(a, b, c), [a, b, c])
#% output
#= [true, false, false, true, false, true, true, false]

#% text
# Some arguments given:

#% cell
boolean_counting_function([2, 3], 4)(true, x, y, z)
#% output
#= boolean_counting_function([1, 2], 3)(x, y, z)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The number of true values over 4 arguments is a sum of binomial coefficients: `1 + 4` for at most one, `6` for exactly two:

#% cell
[count(boolean_table(boolean_counting_function(1, 4)(a, b, c, d), [a, b, c, d]), true), count(boolean_table(boolean_counting_function([[2]], 4)(a, b, c, d), [a, b, c, d]), true), binomial(4, 2)]
#% output
#= [5, 6, 6]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Without arguments the function stays as an operator; a negative bound or an inverted range does not evaluate:

#% cell
[boolean_counting_function(0, 3), boolean_counting_function(-1, 3), boolean_counting_function([2, 1], 3)(a, b, c)]
#% output
#= [boolean_counting_function(0, 3), boolean_counting_function(-1, 3), boolean_counting_function([2, 1], 3)(a, b, c)]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `boolean_function`, `boolean_convert`
